The Road of Trials 
The transformative act for À, then, ž seeking î 
good and advancement and facing her terror of being alone. The 
transformative act for a man is often putting aside his terror of being 
swallowed up by ¥ connectedness and risking genuine intimacy. 
— CAROL S. PEARSON, Awakening the Heroes Within 
The õ crosses the threshold, leaves her parents’ home, and goes in search 
¶ self. In all heroic quests there is a call: when À has outgrown her 
old reality, something enters in to invite her t to move beyond the safety of t y ¢ 
known self, It is a call to growth, and it can bring profound suffering. She is 
“challenged to create ¬ anew č undertake the challenge no matter how 
frightened she may feel. The fear and ã of inadequacy that surface when 
one responds to a call ¹ reason that many of us spend years successfully 
ignoring the urge for growth. 
After crossing the threshold, the õ gathers allies, confronts adversar- 
ies, and begins her road of trials. In early adulthood, this outer road of trials will 
take her ế developmental tasks of adulthood ń culture. Achiev- 
